WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.140
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:09.930
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:12.680
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:12.690 --> 00:00:14.360
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:17.010
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.950
things about the world at the same

00:00:18.950 --> 00:00:20.839
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.500 --> 00:00:24.790
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we

00:00:24.820 --> 00:00:26.630
are going to be talking about the

00:00:26.630 --> 00:00:29.959
American vice president, JD Vance.

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:33.650
Love him or loathe him, he is important,

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.889
and by the time you listen to this

00:00:35.929 --> 00:00:39.259
episode, perhaps he will be even more so.

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:43.140
So today we will take a look at

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:45.970
his life, where he came from, how

00:00:45.970 --> 00:00:48.640
he says this shaped his political

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:52.000
views, and his journey so far almost

00:00:52.059 --> 00:00:54.209
to the top of American politics.

00:00:54.698 --> 00:00:56.678
OK then, let's not waste a

00:00:56.688 --> 00:00:59.208
minute and get right into it.

00:01:00.668 --> 00:01:04.348
In 1937, George Orwell published

00:01:04.438 --> 00:01:06.458
The Road to Wigan Pier.

00:01:07.338 --> 00:01:09.968
In it, he describes the harsh

00:01:10.238 --> 00:01:12.608
day-to-day lives of the working

00:01:12.608 --> 00:01:14.548
class in the North of England.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.748
From details about the mechanics of

00:01:17.748 --> 00:01:20.518
washing clothes right through to the

00:01:20.528 --> 00:01:22.868
daily struggle to get enough money

00:01:22.878 --> 00:01:25.518
to feed their families, it shone a

00:01:25.528 --> 00:01:28.848
light on the bleak reality of life

00:01:29.008 --> 00:01:31.098
in industrial towns and cities.

00:01:32.291 --> 00:01:34.948
To his middle-class readers sipping

00:01:34.948 --> 00:01:37.478
their tea and sitting on comfortable

00:01:37.518 --> 00:01:40.278
armchairs, the book pulled back the

00:01:40.278 --> 00:01:43.728
curtain on a world they knew existed,

00:01:43.948 --> 00:01:47.541
but understood effectively nothing about.

00:01:49.008 --> 00:01:51.848
And in the second half of the book,

00:01:52.368 --> 00:01:55.498
Orwell turned on his own readers.

00:01:56.188 --> 00:01:59.388
He argued that the very people most

00:01:59.398 --> 00:02:02.018
likely to be reading the book — who were

00:02:02.378 --> 00:02:06.158
educated, comfortable, and said that

00:02:06.158 --> 00:02:07.968
they supported better social welfare

00:02:08.518 --> 00:02:11.638
— these were precisely the people who

00:02:11.638 --> 00:02:13.628
were standing in the way of change.

00:02:14.578 --> 00:02:16.478
It wasn’t because they had bad

00:02:16.478 --> 00:02:19.028
intentions, but because of something

00:02:19.338 --> 00:02:22.758
more uncomfortable: they found the

00:02:22.758 --> 00:02:26.008
working class sort of disgusting, their

00:02:26.008 --> 00:02:28.918
smell, their manners, their way of life.

00:02:29.998 --> 00:02:33.618
Orwell’s readers might claim to support

00:02:33.778 --> 00:02:36.538
liberal, even socialist policies,

00:02:36.758 --> 00:02:39.358
but quietly they were physically

00:02:39.578 --> 00:02:42.348
repulsed by the lives of the people

00:02:42.768 --> 00:02:44.718
these policies were meant to help.

00:02:46.368 --> 00:02:49.518
His editor suggested Orwell remove

00:02:49.578 --> 00:02:52.168
the entire second half altogether.

00:02:53.198 --> 00:02:54.438
Orwell refused.

00:02:55.748 --> 00:02:58.718
The book became something of a window

00:02:58.958 --> 00:03:01.098
into a world people said they cared

00:03:01.098 --> 00:03:04.028
about, that politicians said they

00:03:04.028 --> 00:03:07.218
represented, but which Orwell contested

00:03:07.488 --> 00:03:09.748
they had no real understanding of.

00:03:11.148 --> 00:03:15.018
This book, and the idea in the second half

00:03:15.078 --> 00:03:18.278
of it, well it never really went away.

00:03:18.988 --> 00:03:22.078
Every generation or so, when something

00:03:22.108 --> 00:03:24.238
happens that makes the comfortable

00:03:24.238 --> 00:03:26.508
classes realise they understood

00:03:26.508 --> 00:03:28.498
their country far less than they

00:03:28.508 --> 00:03:30.948
thought, someone picks it up again.

00:03:31.508 --> 00:03:33.888
It happened after Margaret Thatcher.

00:03:34.118 --> 00:03:35.448
It happened after Brexit.

00:03:36.278 --> 00:03:38.808
Each time, it serves as a reminder

00:03:38.808 --> 00:03:41.618
that there are whole worlds within

00:03:41.618 --> 00:03:44.258
a society that most people simply

00:03:44.278 --> 00:03:47.618
do not see or understand, especially

00:03:47.828 --> 00:03:50.548
those in positions of political power.

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:55.271
Almost exactly eighty years after the

00:03:55.281 --> 00:03:58.001
publication of The Road to Wigan Pier,

00:03:58.461 --> 00:04:01.721
in 2016, on the other side of the

00:04:01.731 --> 00:04:04.211
Atlantic, another book was published.

00:04:05.371 --> 00:04:09.181
It was called Hillbilly Elegy, and

00:04:09.391 --> 00:04:12.851
was the memoir of a then 31-year-old

00:04:13.091 --> 00:04:16.711
investment professional called JD Vance.

00:04:17.711 --> 00:04:20.981
Now, JD Vance is no Orwell but

00:04:21.301 --> 00:04:23.081
he does tell quite a good story.

00:04:23.851 --> 00:04:27.321
And the story was his own: a chaotic

00:04:27.331 --> 00:04:30.351
childhood, a mother with drug problems,

00:04:30.461 --> 00:04:32.931
his struggle to break out of a system

00:04:33.151 --> 00:04:36.161
that seemed to drag so many others down.

00:04:37.301 --> 00:04:39.501
It was also a political commentary

00:04:39.731 --> 00:04:43.001
on a part of America that he said had

00:04:43.001 --> 00:04:46.151
been left behind: the Rust Belt, which

00:04:46.151 --> 00:04:48.701
is the stretch of the northeastern

00:04:48.871 --> 00:04:51.141
and midwestern United States that

00:04:51.141 --> 00:04:53.411
was once the industrial heartland,

00:04:53.901 --> 00:04:56.181
but has been in serious decline.

00:04:57.311 --> 00:05:00.241
States like Ohio, Pennsylvania,

00:05:00.561 --> 00:05:02.311
Michigan, and Indiana.

00:05:03.731 --> 00:05:05.721
It was also an attempt to explain

00:05:05.921 --> 00:05:08.091
what had happened to communities

00:05:08.091 --> 00:05:09.801
like the one he came from.

00:05:10.261 --> 00:05:12.901
Why had they struggled so much?

00:05:12.991 --> 00:05:16.051
Why was violence so endemic?

00:05:16.471 --> 00:05:18.151
Why had they been particularly

00:05:18.161 --> 00:05:20.721
devastated by the opioid crisis?

00:05:20.981 --> 00:05:23.431
Why did so many young men end

00:05:23.431 --> 00:05:26.151
up in prison or without jobs?

00:05:27.591 --> 00:05:31.871
This book was published in June 2016,

00:05:32.311 --> 00:05:35.241
and, as you may remember, this was just

00:05:35.431 --> 00:05:38.571
as a rather unusual presidential election

00:05:38.751 --> 00:05:41.301
was reaching its most intense phase.

00:05:42.151 --> 00:05:44.591
A year beforehand, Donald Trump

00:05:44.881 --> 00:05:47.411
had announced his candidacy for the

00:05:47.461 --> 00:05:50.231
presidency, and he had been winning

00:05:50.231 --> 00:05:52.811
support in precisely the kind of

00:05:52.811 --> 00:05:54.951
communities Vance wrote about.

00:05:55.711 --> 00:05:58.471
Trump claimed to understand these

00:05:58.471 --> 00:06:01.241
communities, but he made no claim

00:06:01.521 --> 00:06:04.381
to be like them; his entire brand

00:06:04.661 --> 00:06:07.701
was about wealth and power, New York

00:06:07.701 --> 00:06:10.411
penthouses and golden chandeliers.

00:06:11.451 --> 00:06:14.351
Suddenly, journalists and television

00:06:14.381 --> 00:06:17.451
producers needed someone to explain

00:06:17.481 --> 00:06:19.761
these communities to the rest of America.

00:06:20.681 --> 00:06:22.991
JD Vance, with his unusual

00:06:23.011 --> 00:06:25.931
combination of Yale degree and

00:06:25.941 --> 00:06:28.411
Rust Belt background, well, he was

00:06:28.411 --> 00:06:31.071
perfectly placed to do exactly that.

00:06:32.181 --> 00:06:35.531
Almost overnight, JD Vance became a

00:06:35.531 --> 00:06:38.371
national figure — someone whose voice

00:06:38.391 --> 00:06:40.781
was sought out whenever people wanted

00:06:40.781 --> 00:06:43.141
to understand what was happening in

00:06:43.141 --> 00:06:45.091
those forgotten corners of America.

00:06:46.541 --> 00:06:49.481
The publicity turbocharged his book,

00:06:49.751 --> 00:06:52.411
and it became a massive bestseller.

00:06:53.111 --> 00:06:55.721
It spent well over a year on the

00:06:55.731 --> 00:06:57.521
New York Times bestseller list.

00:06:57.731 --> 00:06:59.251
It was translated into

00:06:59.281 --> 00:07:00.651
dozens of languages.

00:07:01.131 --> 00:07:03.921
And in 2020, it would get its own

00:07:03.931 --> 00:07:07.081
film version, directed by Ron Howard.

00:07:08.061 --> 00:07:12.631
And then on the 15th of July, 2024, as

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:14.881
part of his campaign for re-election,

00:07:15.451 --> 00:07:17.211
Donald Trump would announce that

00:07:17.221 --> 00:07:19.871
his running mate and candidate for

00:07:19.871 --> 00:07:21.991
Vice President would be none other

00:07:22.301 --> 00:07:25.741
than the then 39-year-old JD Vance.

00:07:26.731 --> 00:07:30.591
But to understand why Trump chose

00:07:30.591 --> 00:07:33.541
him, and why the choice made sense

00:07:33.551 --> 00:07:35.531
to so many Americans, we need

00:07:35.531 --> 00:07:37.551
to understand where he started.

00:07:38.521 --> 00:07:42.485
His name at birth, in August 1984,

00:07:42.791 --> 00:07:46.631
was James Donald Bowman, and he was

00:07:46.631 --> 00:07:49.431
born and raised in Middletown, Ohio.

00:07:50.151 --> 00:07:52.341
His family background was

00:07:52.381 --> 00:07:54.661
complicated from the very beginning.

00:07:54.991 --> 00:07:56.891
His parents divorced when

00:07:56.891 --> 00:07:58.031
he was still a toddler.

00:07:58.361 --> 00:07:59.761
His father all but

00:07:59.771 --> 00:08:01.341
disappeared from his life.

00:08:01.711 --> 00:08:03.881
His mother struggled deeply

00:08:03.881 --> 00:08:05.551
with drug addiction, both

00:08:05.741 --> 00:08:08.125
heroin and prescription opioids.

00:08:08.881 --> 00:08:12.861
And his early life was unstable, often

00:08:12.921 --> 00:08:15.431
unhappy, and at times, frightening.

00:08:16.111 --> 00:08:17.701
He writes about this very

00:08:17.741 --> 00:08:19.961
openly in Hillbilly Elegy.

00:08:20.411 --> 00:08:22.651
He described living through multiple

00:08:22.651 --> 00:08:26.191
stepfathers, the constant instability, and

00:08:26.561 --> 00:08:29.871
real fear, sometimes even for his life.

00:08:30.901 --> 00:08:33.141
There’s a particularly memorable scene

00:08:33.141 --> 00:08:35.751
in the book where his mother is driving

00:08:36.011 --> 00:08:39.051
with him in the backseat and she threatens

00:08:39.101 --> 00:08:42.031
to kill them both by crashing the car.

00:08:43.441 --> 00:08:47.261
But amid all of this chaos, there were

00:08:47.341 --> 00:08:49.941
two people who provided some stability:

00:08:50.325 --> 00:08:52.821
his grandparents, Mamaw and Papaw.

00:08:53.601 --> 00:08:56.331
Mamaw, in particular, is a figure

00:08:56.331 --> 00:08:58.521
who appears again and again in

00:08:58.601 --> 00:09:00.621
everything Vance has written

00:09:00.621 --> 00:09:02.511
or said about his childhood.

00:09:02.981 --> 00:09:05.261
She was tough, fiercely loyal,

00:09:05.471 --> 00:09:08.381
deeply religious in her own way,

00:09:08.661 --> 00:09:10.661
and utterly determined that her

00:09:10.671 --> 00:09:13.311
grandson would not be swallowed up

00:09:13.651 --> 00:09:15.341
by the circumstances around him.

00:09:16.221 --> 00:09:19.051
She made sure he had what

00:09:19.051 --> 00:09:20.341
he needed to go to school.

00:09:20.531 --> 00:09:22.431
She gave him a place to live when

00:09:22.431 --> 00:09:24.751
his home life became unbearable.

00:09:25.081 --> 00:09:27.701
She pushed him to do things that

00:09:27.701 --> 00:09:29.441
he wouldn't have done on his own.

00:09:30.441 --> 00:09:33.951
And by the time Vance was around fifteen

00:09:33.951 --> 00:09:36.391
or sixteen years old, he had moved

00:09:36.461 --> 00:09:38.851
out of his mother's home permanently

00:09:39.071 --> 00:09:40.991
and was living with his grandmother.

00:09:41.771 --> 00:09:44.051
He graduated from high school in

00:09:44.051 --> 00:09:47.701
2003, and rather than going straight

00:09:47.721 --> 00:09:49.861
to university or trying to get a job,

00:09:50.181 --> 00:09:53.031
he enlisted in the military as what’s

00:09:53.031 --> 00:09:55.171
called a “combat correspondent”,

00:09:55.591 --> 00:09:57.991
essentially a military journalist,

00:09:58.111 --> 00:10:00.141
someone who follows the troops,

00:10:00.301 --> 00:10:02.401
takes pictures, and writes articles.

00:10:03.311 --> 00:10:05.371
He served for four years,

00:10:05.681 --> 00:10:07.791
including a tour in Iraq.

00:10:09.171 --> 00:10:11.241
As he would describe in his memoir,

00:10:11.581 --> 00:10:13.711
the military gave him something that

00:10:13.711 --> 00:10:16.621
his childhood had not: structure,

00:10:17.081 --> 00:10:20.011
discipline, and a sense of belonging.

00:10:21.521 --> 00:10:25.051
When he left the military in 2007, he

00:10:25.051 --> 00:10:27.571
used a government scheme called the GI

00:10:27.591 --> 00:10:30.821
Bill to study at Ohio State University.

00:10:31.655 --> 00:10:34.671
At school, his grades had been

00:10:34.691 --> 00:10:37.051
fine, but nothing special.

00:10:37.681 --> 00:10:40.631
At college, or at university as we’d say

00:10:40.631 --> 00:10:44.161
in the UK, something clearly changed.

00:10:44.721 --> 00:10:48.101
He graduated in 2009 with the

00:10:48.111 --> 00:10:50.541
highest honours, with a degree in

00:10:50.541 --> 00:10:52.511
political science and philosophy.

00:10:53.691 --> 00:10:57.081
Now, Ohio State is a well-rated public

00:10:57.091 --> 00:11:00.461
university, but his next step would

00:11:00.471 --> 00:11:03.181
be into a world as foreign to him as

00:11:03.181 --> 00:11:05.131
the world of the Lancashire miners

00:11:05.341 --> 00:11:08.181
was to a doctor from Bristol: he

00:11:08.181 --> 00:11:11.141
was accepted into Yale Law School.

00:11:12.421 --> 00:11:14.281
Yale Law School is not

00:11:14.281 --> 00:11:15.901
just any university.

00:11:16.171 --> 00:11:17.731
It's one of the most prestigious

00:11:17.731 --> 00:11:20.591
universities in the United States, a place

00:11:20.781 --> 00:11:23.861
where Supreme Court justices, senators,

00:11:24.101 --> 00:11:26.081
and presidents have passed through.

00:11:27.141 --> 00:11:30.561
For a kid from small-town Ohio, whose

00:11:30.561 --> 00:11:32.651
mother had battled addiction and

00:11:32.651 --> 00:11:34.711
whose childhood had been defined by

00:11:34.731 --> 00:11:37.811
instability, getting into Yale was,

00:11:38.241 --> 00:11:40.141
to put it simply, extraordinary.

00:11:41.411 --> 00:11:43.691
And Vance said that when he first

00:11:43.741 --> 00:11:47.011
arrived, he felt completely out of place.

00:11:47.891 --> 00:11:50.461
He was surrounded by people who had gone

00:11:50.461 --> 00:11:53.221
to elite private schools, whose parents

00:11:53.441 --> 00:11:56.551
were surgeons and lawyers and politicians.

00:11:57.091 --> 00:11:59.761
He did not know the unwritten rules.

00:11:59.971 --> 00:12:01.651
He did not know which fork

00:12:01.651 --> 00:12:03.471
to use at a formal dinner.

00:12:03.871 --> 00:12:05.861
He did not speak the language of the

00:12:05.861 --> 00:12:07.791
professional class in the way his

00:12:07.791 --> 00:12:10.601
classmates seemed to do effortlessly.

00:12:11.551 --> 00:12:14.111
But he was smart, he worked

00:12:14.111 --> 00:12:16.651
hard, and he had something his

00:12:16.701 --> 00:12:19.641
classmates often lacked: a story.

00:12:20.171 --> 00:12:23.986
A real, raw, lived experience of a part

00:12:23.986 --> 00:12:26.391
of America that most of his professors

00:12:26.601 --> 00:12:28.951
and fellow students had never seen.

00:12:30.205 --> 00:12:32.421
At Yale, he met two women

00:12:32.691 --> 00:12:34.151
who would change his life.

00:12:35.181 --> 00:12:37.931
The first was a fellow student, and the

00:12:37.931 --> 00:12:40.551
woman who would become his wife, Usha.

00:12:41.281 --> 00:12:43.041
She was the daughter of Indian

00:12:43.051 --> 00:12:45.401
immigrants and was herself a

00:12:45.431 --> 00:12:46.991
highly accomplished lawyer.

00:12:48.061 --> 00:12:50.671
The second was a professor named

00:12:50.741 --> 00:12:53.801
Amy Chua, whose name you might

00:12:53.811 --> 00:12:55.781
recognise from her book about being

00:12:55.781 --> 00:12:59.101
a Tiger Mum, and how children benefit

00:12:59.321 --> 00:13:01.911
from being pushed to study, play

00:13:01.911 --> 00:13:03.531
musical instruments, and so on.

00:13:04.561 --> 00:13:07.561
Chua had published her book, Battle

00:13:07.581 --> 00:13:11.195
Hymn of the Tiger Mother, in 2011.

00:13:11.721 --> 00:13:14.611
It quickly became a bestseller, and

00:13:14.831 --> 00:13:16.941
had brought the already successful

00:13:16.961 --> 00:13:19.171
Chua countless opportunities.

00:13:20.231 --> 00:13:23.411
She knew Vance’s backstory, and

00:13:23.641 --> 00:13:26.861
encouraged him to write about his life.

00:13:27.751 --> 00:13:28.251
He did.

00:13:28.721 --> 00:13:31.011
And the result was Hillbilly

00:13:31.051 --> 00:13:33.701
Elegy, a book that opened doors

00:13:33.901 --> 00:13:36.251
Vance didn’t even know existed.

00:13:37.571 --> 00:13:40.771
Now, after graduating from Yale, he

00:13:40.771 --> 00:13:43.641
spent a few years in the legal world,

00:13:44.011 --> 00:13:46.451
first working for a district judge,

00:13:46.711 --> 00:13:48.691
and then at a corporate law firm.

00:13:49.401 --> 00:13:51.941
But being a lawyer, he soon

00:13:51.941 --> 00:13:53.801
discovered, wasn’t for him.

00:13:54.731 --> 00:13:57.321
He moved to California and started

00:13:57.321 --> 00:13:58.831
working in venture capital,

00:13:59.341 --> 00:14:01.461
investing in technology startups.

00:14:02.401 --> 00:14:04.601
He eventually ended up working for

00:14:04.601 --> 00:14:07.791
a firm backed by Peter Thiel, the

00:14:07.801 --> 00:14:10.681
billionaire tech investor, who became

00:14:10.681 --> 00:14:12.771
something of a mentor to Vance.

00:14:13.731 --> 00:14:16.191
Now, Thiel is a fascinating

00:14:16.381 --> 00:14:17.921
and controversial figure.

00:14:18.771 --> 00:14:21.141
He was one of the founders of PayPal,

00:14:21.571 --> 00:14:23.301
an early investor in Facebook,

00:14:23.481 --> 00:14:26.031
and has accumulated enormous

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:28.201
wealth and political influence.

00:14:29.211 --> 00:14:31.441
He is also someone who has been quite

00:14:31.491 --> 00:14:34.501
open about his desire to reshape

00:14:34.731 --> 00:14:38.014
American politics in a more nationalist,

00:14:38.281 --> 00:14:40.061
anti-establishment direction.

00:14:41.391 --> 00:14:43.411
When Vance launched his political

00:14:43.421 --> 00:14:45.881
career, Thiel provided much

00:14:45.901 --> 00:14:47.141
of the financial backing.

00:14:47.791 --> 00:14:50.401
Vance was working for Thiel when he

00:14:50.411 --> 00:14:52.581
published his bestselling memoir.

00:14:53.911 --> 00:14:56.501
For Vance's critics, this

00:14:56.521 --> 00:14:58.471
raises an obvious question.

00:14:59.271 --> 00:15:02.001
Here is a man who presents himself as

00:15:02.001 --> 00:15:04.981
the champion of forgotten, working-class

00:15:04.981 --> 00:15:08.031
Americans, and yet his political career

00:15:08.031 --> 00:15:10.591
and success were largely bankrolled

00:15:10.811 --> 00:15:12.941
by one of the wealthiest and most

00:15:12.981 --> 00:15:15.121
powerful figures in Silicon Valley.

00:15:15.811 --> 00:15:17.711
The very world, in other words,

00:15:17.901 --> 00:15:20.311
that Vance himself has described

00:15:20.371 --> 00:15:22.161
as a self-serving elite.

00:15:23.261 --> 00:15:25.531
Whether that is a damning contradiction

00:15:25.951 --> 00:15:28.441
or simply the reality of how American

00:15:28.441 --> 00:15:31.221
politics is funded, well, that’s something

00:15:31.221 --> 00:15:32.791
you can make up your own mind about.

00:15:34.041 --> 00:15:36.421
Now, to understand how the

00:15:36.431 --> 00:15:38.671
political career began, we need to

00:15:38.671 --> 00:15:41.341
go back briefly to the book, and

00:15:41.431 --> 00:15:43.291
what happened in its aftermath.

00:15:44.151 --> 00:15:46.171
We've already talked about the book's

00:15:46.211 --> 00:15:49.331
extraordinary timing and its immediate

00:15:49.441 --> 00:15:52.201
impact, in terms of Vance being called

00:15:52.201 --> 00:15:55.211
upon as the authoritative source on

00:15:55.251 --> 00:15:57.381
understanding the working class America.

00:15:58.091 --> 00:16:00.021
But there is something else

00:16:00.181 --> 00:16:01.561
worth understanding about what

00:16:01.561 --> 00:16:03.661
happened to Vance in the years

00:16:03.661 --> 00:16:04.961
that followed its publication.

00:16:06.581 --> 00:16:09.101
For a time, he occupied

00:16:09.121 --> 00:16:11.161
a curious position.

00:16:12.511 --> 00:16:13.921
He was famous for

00:16:13.981 --> 00:16:15.631
understanding Trump voters.

00:16:15.821 --> 00:16:18.181
He was the man editors and television

00:16:18.181 --> 00:16:20.171
producers called when they needed

00:16:20.171 --> 00:16:22.621
someone to explain them: to give them a

00:16:22.921 --> 00:16:26.331
human face, to make them understandable

00:16:26.411 --> 00:16:28.971
to a coastal, affluent audience.

00:16:29.741 --> 00:16:32.541
But privately, JD Vance

00:16:32.751 --> 00:16:34.981
found Trump reprehensible.

00:16:36.091 --> 00:16:38.531
In messages that later became public,

00:16:38.871 --> 00:16:42.031
he called Trump a morally reprehensible

00:16:42.041 --> 00:16:44.591
human being, and said he went back

00:16:44.601 --> 00:16:47.211
and forth between thinking, and I’m

00:16:47.211 --> 00:16:50.171
quoting directly: “Trump is a cynical

00:16:50.251 --> 00:16:52.471
a--hole like Nixon who wouldn't be

00:16:52.471 --> 00:16:55.171
that bad [and might even prove useful]

00:16:55.611 --> 00:16:58.041
or that he's America's Hitler.”

00:16:59.451 --> 00:17:02.471
He did not vote for Trump in 2016,

00:17:02.781 --> 00:17:05.481
and was a public “Never Trumper”.

00:17:06.531 --> 00:17:09.681
But sometime during Trump’s

00:17:09.691 --> 00:17:12.911
first term, he changed his tune.

00:17:14.321 --> 00:17:17.381
When Vance announced in 2021 that he

00:17:17.381 --> 00:17:19.851
was running for a seat in the United

00:17:19.851 --> 00:17:22.441
States Senate, representing Ohio,

00:17:22.951 --> 00:17:26.001
he ran as a strong Trump supporter.

00:17:26.761 --> 00:17:29.431
He publicly apologised for his earlier

00:17:29.431 --> 00:17:31.711
criticisms, deleted social media

00:17:31.711 --> 00:17:34.041
posts in which he had attacked Trump,

00:17:34.421 --> 00:17:37.411
and asked for Trump's endorsement.

00:17:38.711 --> 00:17:40.131
Trump gave it.

00:17:40.501 --> 00:17:43.551
And with that endorsement, Vance won

00:17:43.571 --> 00:17:46.351
the Republican primary in Ohio and then

00:17:46.351 --> 00:17:50.381
the Senate seat itself in November 2022.

00:17:51.631 --> 00:17:54.351
His critics say this transformation

00:17:54.661 --> 00:17:56.321
tells you everything you need to

00:17:56.321 --> 00:17:58.551
know about his character, that

00:17:58.721 --> 00:18:00.721
he is a deeply opportunistic

00:18:00.721 --> 00:18:02.975
politician who will say whatever

00:18:02.975 --> 00:18:05.221
is necessary to advance his career.

00:18:06.271 --> 00:18:08.461
His supporters say that people are

00:18:08.491 --> 00:18:10.421
allowed to change their minds, that

00:18:10.601 --> 00:18:12.991
Vance genuinely came to believe

00:18:13.171 --> 00:18:14.731
that Trump's instincts about the

00:18:14.731 --> 00:18:17.591
working class were correct, and that

00:18:17.601 --> 00:18:19.771
the transformation represents an

00:18:19.891 --> 00:18:22.021
honest evolution of his thinking.

00:18:23.361 --> 00:18:25.541
I’ll leave that without comment, but

00:18:25.765 --> 00:18:28.031
what is certainly true is that without

00:18:28.101 --> 00:18:30.811
Trump's endorsement, Vance would almost

00:18:30.811 --> 00:18:33.181
certainly not have won the primary.

00:18:34.581 --> 00:18:36.121
The working-class champion

00:18:36.388 --> 00:18:37.701
needed the billionaire's

00:18:37.711 --> 00:18:39.471
blessing to get through the door.

00:18:40.611 --> 00:18:43.901
And as a senator, Vance quickly

00:18:43.911 --> 00:18:45.831
established himself as one of the

00:18:45.911 --> 00:18:48.211
most prominent voices of what is

00:18:48.211 --> 00:18:50.431
sometimes called America First

00:18:50.471 --> 00:18:53.191
conservatism, or national conservatism.

00:18:54.001 --> 00:18:56.691
So, what does that mean in practice?

00:18:57.231 --> 00:19:00.861
Well, the overarching theme is, as

00:19:00.861 --> 00:19:03.361
the term suggests, to put American

00:19:03.551 --> 00:19:07.001
interests first, both at home and abroad.

00:19:08.171 --> 00:19:10.771
On foreign policy, Vance has been

00:19:10.771 --> 00:19:12.981
consistent in arguing that the United

00:19:12.981 --> 00:19:16.041
States spends too much money and political

00:19:16.061 --> 00:19:19.041
energy on foreign conflicts — particularly

00:19:19.041 --> 00:19:21.671
in Europe and the Middle East — when it

00:19:21.691 --> 00:19:24.381
should be focusing on problems at home.

00:19:25.181 --> 00:19:27.071
He has been one of the most prominent

00:19:27.141 --> 00:19:30.791
sceptics of American aid to Ukraine, and

00:19:30.981 --> 00:19:33.901
has argued that Europe needs to take far

00:19:33.901 --> 00:19:36.871
greater responsibility for its own defence

00:19:37.071 --> 00:19:39.411
rather than relying on American support.

00:19:40.141 --> 00:19:43.031
On the economy, he positions himself as

00:19:43.041 --> 00:19:46.101
someone who speaks on behalf of ordinary

00:19:46.121 --> 00:19:49.851
working people against what he sees as a

00:19:49.861 --> 00:19:52.891
self-serving elite class of politicians,

00:19:53.251 --> 00:19:55.201
journalists, and business leaders.

00:19:55.981 --> 00:19:57.641
He has been more willing than many

00:19:57.641 --> 00:19:59.811
traditional conservatives to support

00:19:59.851 --> 00:20:01.771
measures that protect American

00:20:01.771 --> 00:20:05.241
manufacturing, even when those conflict

00:20:05.391 --> 00:20:07.471
with the free trade principles his

00:20:07.471 --> 00:20:09.511
party has historically championed.

00:20:10.491 --> 00:20:13.811
On social issues, he is very conservative.

00:20:14.131 --> 00:20:17.091
He is anti-abortion, and has spoken

00:20:17.111 --> 00:20:19.631
extensively about the importance of

00:20:19.631 --> 00:20:22.931
what he calls family formation, and the

00:20:22.931 --> 00:20:25.221
danger of falling birth rates in America.

00:20:25.781 --> 00:20:28.351
Essentially he is all about encouraging

00:20:28.351 --> 00:20:30.661
people to get married and have children.

00:20:31.871 --> 00:20:34.681
On immigration, he has taken a very strict

00:20:34.711 --> 00:20:37.321
position, supporting tight restrictions

00:20:37.531 --> 00:20:40.301
on both legal and illegal immigration.

00:20:41.341 --> 00:20:44.621
And on matters of culture and free

00:20:44.621 --> 00:20:46.791
expression, he has been increasingly

00:20:46.821 --> 00:20:49.571
critical of what he sees as the

00:20:49.571 --> 00:20:51.871
suppression of conservative voices,

00:20:52.151 --> 00:20:54.991
both in the United States and in Europe.

00:20:56.071 --> 00:20:58.111
You might remember his speech at the

00:20:58.121 --> 00:21:01.391
Munich Security Conference in 2025,

00:21:01.801 --> 00:21:04.781
where rather than reassuring European

00:21:04.791 --> 00:21:07.441
allies, he spent his time warning

00:21:07.441 --> 00:21:09.771
them that the real threat to their

00:21:09.811 --> 00:21:12.851
continent came not from Russia or

00:21:12.851 --> 00:21:15.661
China, but from their own leaders.

00:21:16.421 --> 00:21:18.351
Now, we’re not going to make this

00:21:18.361 --> 00:21:21.761
episode too much about the politics of

00:21:21.761 --> 00:21:24.691
JD Vance, and what he has and hasn’t

00:21:24.691 --> 00:21:26.861
done since becoming Vice President.

00:21:27.451 --> 00:21:29.561
That’s something of an impossible

00:21:29.591 --> 00:21:32.031
task, and no doubt by the time you

00:21:32.051 --> 00:21:34.381
listen to this, something new and

00:21:34.411 --> 00:21:36.181
unpredictable will have happened.

00:21:36.961 --> 00:21:38.441
Instead, we are going to

00:21:38.441 --> 00:21:40.341
look forward, to the future.

00:21:41.651 --> 00:21:43.851
Donald Trump, under the American

00:21:43.891 --> 00:21:46.271
Constitution, cannot serve more

00:21:46.271 --> 00:21:48.131
than two terms as President.

00:21:48.921 --> 00:21:51.301
His second term began in January

00:21:51.511 --> 00:21:55.971
2025 and will end in January 2029.

00:21:56.641 --> 00:21:58.891
After that, he cannot run again.

00:21:59.901 --> 00:22:03.321
This means that the 2028 presidential

00:22:03.331 --> 00:22:05.851
election will be an open contest

00:22:06.041 --> 00:22:07.411
for the Republican Party.

00:22:08.331 --> 00:22:12.331
And as of early 2026, JD Vance

00:22:12.401 --> 00:22:14.641
is widely seen as the most

00:22:14.651 --> 00:22:16.661
likely Republican candidate.

00:22:17.151 --> 00:22:19.201
The most recent polls have him

00:22:19.201 --> 00:22:22.271
with a 45% chance of becoming the

00:22:22.271 --> 00:22:24.571
Republican nominee, with Donald

00:22:24.571 --> 00:22:28.161
Trump Jr trailing behind at 17%.

00:22:29.281 --> 00:22:32.411
Clearly, a lot can change and no doubt

00:22:32.421 --> 00:22:35.081
will change between now and November

00:22:35.081 --> 00:22:38.831
2028, but were Vance to be nominated,

00:22:39.201 --> 00:22:41.521
and were he to win, he would become

00:22:41.531 --> 00:22:43.781
the President of the United States at

00:22:43.791 --> 00:22:47.031
the age of just 44, making him one of

00:22:47.031 --> 00:22:49.471
the youngest presidents in US history.

00:22:50.551 --> 00:22:53.711
So, to bring us back to where we started.

00:22:54.261 --> 00:22:56.601
George Orwell's great question — the

00:22:56.601 --> 00:22:59.121
one his editor wanted to cut, the

00:22:59.121 --> 00:23:01.291
one that made The Road to Wigan Pier

00:23:01.511 --> 00:23:04.481
uncomfortable to its readers — was whether

00:23:04.481 --> 00:23:07.241
the people who claimed to speak for the

00:23:07.241 --> 00:23:09.621
working class actually understood them.

00:23:10.621 --> 00:23:12.731
Whether they had earned that right.

00:23:12.731 --> 00:23:15.301
Whether their sympathy was real,

00:23:15.531 --> 00:23:17.741
or whether it was something more

00:23:17.741 --> 00:23:20.231
convenient and more self-serving.

00:23:21.391 --> 00:23:22.971
A similar question can

00:23:22.971 --> 00:23:25.361
certainly be asked of JD Vance.

00:23:25.961 --> 00:23:27.691
He grew up in Middletown.

00:23:27.981 --> 00:23:30.791
He knows what a community looks like when

00:23:30.791 --> 00:23:33.461
the jobs disappear and don't come back.

00:23:34.141 --> 00:23:37.511
But he also went to Yale, worked for

00:23:37.511 --> 00:23:39.931
Peter Thiel, and is now one of the

00:23:39.931 --> 00:23:42.381
most powerful people in the world.

00:23:43.451 --> 00:23:46.121
His supporters see that journey, from a

00:23:46.131 --> 00:23:49.221
chaotic childhood in small-town Ohio to

00:23:49.221 --> 00:23:51.161
the second-highest office in the land,

00:23:51.831 --> 00:23:54.611
they see this as proof that the American

00:23:54.621 --> 00:23:58.451
dream is still real, and exists in a man

00:23:58.451 --> 00:24:00.811
who will support and champion them in a

00:24:00.811 --> 00:24:03.411
way that the coastal elites never will.

00:24:04.361 --> 00:24:07.281
His critics see the same journey and

00:24:07.321 --> 00:24:10.541
ask other questions: was the Trump

00:24:10.551 --> 00:24:13.171
reversal an honest change of heart,

00:24:13.531 --> 00:24:15.541
or a calculated political move?

00:24:16.051 --> 00:24:18.571
And can a man whose career was

00:24:18.571 --> 00:24:20.721
funded by one of Silicon Valley's

00:24:20.791 --> 00:24:23.031
wealthiest investors really be

00:24:23.031 --> 00:24:24.971
the champion of forgotten America?

00:24:26.051 --> 00:24:29.051
These are not questions with easy answers.

00:24:29.641 --> 00:24:32.781
And whatever you make of him, the story

00:24:32.981 --> 00:24:35.951
of JD Vance is still being written.

00:24:37.571 --> 00:24:38.321
OK then.

00:24:38.561 --> 00:24:40.391
That is it for today's episode on

00:24:40.461 --> 00:24:43.151
JD Vance — the man from Middletown,

00:24:43.151 --> 00:24:45.561
Ohio, who might one day be the

00:24:45.561 --> 00:24:47.341
most powerful person on earth.

00:24:47.821 --> 00:24:49.501
As always, I would love to know

00:24:49.501 --> 00:24:50.991
what you thought of this episode.

00:24:51.311 --> 00:24:53.411
What do you think about JD Vance?

00:24:53.691 --> 00:24:56.511
And what do you think his story tells us?

00:24:56.881 --> 00:24:58.121
You can head right into our

00:24:58.121 --> 00:24:59.771
community forum, which is at

00:24:59.771 --> 00:25:02.421
community.leonardoenglish.com and get

00:25:02.421 --> 00:25:04.551
chatting away to other curious minds.

00:25:04.961 --> 00:25:06.791
You've been listening to English Learning

00:25:06.791 --> 00:25:09.391
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:25:09.731 --> 00:25:12.291
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:25:12.291 --> 00:25:14.711
I'll catch you in the next episode.

